      - Analysis: {"status":"NEEDS_IMPROVEMENT","core_findings":["Electronics has the weakest positive–negative separation: 14.95% of sampled negatives outrank positives, and its 10th-percentile margin is negative (-0.4812). Movies_and_TV separates these pairs most reliably, with only 3.03% wrong rankings.","Industrial_and_Scientific and CDs_and_Vinyl show intermediate weaknesses, with wrong-ranking rates of 8.22% and 9.14%. Embedding similarity scores of 0.5392–0.5566 indicate modest directional alignment, without strong evidence of severe collapse."],"key_implications":["Ranking errors are uneven across datasets, with Electronics showing the greatest difficulty on sampled training pairs.","Positive mean margins coexist with weak lower-tail separation, so average separation masks confusing cases.","These training diagnostics alone do not establish validation HR@10 or generalization."],"evidence":{"headline_metrics":{"Electronics:pos_neg_neg_beats_pos_rate":0.1495,"Electronics:pos_neg_margin_p10":-0.4812,"Movies_and_TV:pos_neg_neg_beats_pos_rate":0.0303,"Industrial_and_Scientific:embedding_collapse_score":0.5566},"brief_metric_read":{"Electronics:pos_neg_neg_beats_pos_rate":"Highest fraction of negatives outranking positives.","Electronics:pos_neg_margin_p10":"Lower-tail margins fall below zero.","Movies_and_TV:pos_neg_neg_beats_pos_rate":"Lowest observed wrong-ranking rate.","Industrial_and_Scientific:embedding_collapse_score":"Highest embedding alignment score, still close to the 0.5 midpoint."}}}
    
