2026-10-23 08:00:21,873 self_evolverec INFO   Report 1: At 20% progress, prioritize a small, testable extension: **a bounded directed-transition residual supported by multiple recent historical items**, compared with the starting lexical residual and an objective-only control. This is a proposal; no new performance is established.

**Insights from the starting point.** (1) Repeated support can reduce reliance on isolated purchases. Distinct item IDs must provide support, since repetitions of one ID offer weaker evidence. (2) Recency weighting addresses stale interests. Confidence must multiply the normalized residual afterward so normalization cannot erase weak support. (3) Fixed lexical features offer inexpensive, inspectable evidence. Shared vocabulary nevertheless cannot establish practical usefulness or compatibility.

**Insights from related work.** (1) Collaboration and Transition [1] motivates directional next-item associations. These can distinguish observed behavioral progression from symmetric similarity without asserting complementarity. (2) Long/short-term modeling [2] motivates selecting persistent interests using recent context. A short recency window is an inexpensive approximation, although it may miss infrequent interests. (3) eSASRec [3] motivates an objective-only sampled-softmax control. Electronics’ 16.04% negative-over-positive training-pair rate justifies investigating discrimination, but does not identify its cause.

**Framework and directions.** Organize methods along evidence, temporal selection, and optimization:

| Idea | Evidence / temporal selection | O/F/U/M/D |
|---|---|---|
| A: Objective-only SASRec | Collaborative / existing attention | 2/6/3/8/2 |
| B: Starting lexical residual | Symmetric text / recent repeated support | 4/6/7/6/4 |
| C: Directed residual, selected | Next-item transitions / recent distinct-source support | 4/7/8/7/4 |

Scores are prospective: originality, future potential, user alignment, model alignment, and implementation difficulty; higher difficulty means harder. A offers clean attribution but weak direct coverage of user failures. B offers inspectable specificity but can reward boilerplate. C offers directional evidence but suffers sparse counts and popularity confounding. Its originality is modest, its potential is testable, and its implementation requires careful causal counting.

**Selected algorithm.** Count adjacent transitions from training histories. Smooth each destination distribution toward training destination popularity, then compute clipped log-lift. For each causal prefix, aggregate positive lift from distinct items among its latest 20 events, weighting each by recency and count reliability. Activate the bounded residual only when at least two distinct sources support the candidate.

This explicitly targets broad associations lacking directional evidence and older or isolated interactions outweighing repeated recent interests. Uniform 32-negative sampled softmax targets weak training-pair separation. Behavioral associations remain insufficient to establish compatibility, ownership, or unmet needs.

Compare A, B, and C under the unchanged four-category protocol and shared budget; select solely by pinned macro validation HR@10. Preserve seeds, routing, diagnostics, and generated-predictor parity. Track residual activation, ranking changes, and category-level validation results. Keep simulator judgments about practical fit separate from sampled HR@10.

References: [1] [Collaboration and Transition](https://arxiv.org/abs/2311.01056); [2] [Hierarchical long/short-term modeling](https://journal.hep.com.cn/fcs/EN/10.1007/s11704-025-41181-y); [3] [eSASRec](https://arxiv.org/abs/2508.06450).
2026-10-23 08:00:21,873 self_evolverec INFO   Report 2: At 20% progress, prioritize a small, testable extension: **an edge-reliability-weighted directed-transition residual**, with multiple-source gating tested separately. Compare it with objective-only SASRec and the starting lexical residual. No new performance is established.

**Insights from the starting point.** (1) Distinct-item support can limit isolated-purchase effects. Repeated events from one item must not count as independent sources. (2) Recency weighting addresses stale evidence. Apply confidence after normalization so weak support remains weak. (3) Fixed lexical features provide inspectable specificity. Boilerplate and shared vocabulary nevertheless cannot establish usefulness or compatibility.

**Insights from related work.** (1) Collaboration and Transition [1] motivates directional associations. These encode behavioral progression without establishing complementarity. (2) Long/short-term modeling [2] motivates selecting persistent interests using recent context. A short window approximates this cheaply but can miss infrequent interests. (3) eSASRec [3] motivates an objective-only control. Electronics’ 16.04% negative-over-positive training-pair rate warrants investigation without diagnosing its cause. (4) SpotTarget [4] distinguishes training-target inclusion from held-out leakage. User-excluded graph diagnostics can expose self-contributed evidence, although frozen-weight ablations cannot remove parameter memorization. (5) Dunning [5] motivates caution about rare associations. Source frequency alone cannot establish individual-edge reliability.

**Framework and directions.** Separate evidence, temporal selection, reliability, and optimization:

| Idea | Evidence / temporal selection | O/F/U/M/D |
|---|---|---|
| A: Objective-only SASRec | Collaborative / existing attention | 2/6/3/8/2 |
| B: Starting lexical residual | Symmetric text / recent repeated support | 4/6/7/6/4 |
| C: Directed residual, selected family | Transitions / recent sources and distinct-user shrinkage | 3/6/7/7/5 |

Scores are prospective: originality, future potential, user alignment, model alignment, and implementation difficulty. A offers clean attribution but limited direct user-failure coverage. B offers specificity but risks boilerplate shortcuts. C offers directional evidence but risks rare-edge noise, popularity effects, and self-contributed training support. Its contribution is an incremental combination; efficient sparse scoring and diagnostic parity raise implementation difficulty.

**Selected algorithm.** Count training-only adjacent transitions and distinct contributing users per edge. Smooth destination probabilities toward training popularity, clip positive log-lift, and shrink each edge by distinct-user support. Aggregate across deduplicated recent sources with recency and source-reliability weights. Compare the bounded residual with and without the original two-source gate; do not presume gating improves sparse histories.

This targets broad associations lacking directional support and stale or isolated interactions outweighing repeated recent interests. Uniform 32-negative sampled softmax targets weak training-pair separation. Neither mechanism establishes compatibility, ownership, or unmet needs.

Compare A, B, ungated C, and gated C under the remaining shared budget. Preserve all four categories, seeds, routing, diagnostics, and predictor parity; select exclusively by pinned macro validation HR@10. Track coverage, support buckets, rank changes, and user-excluded graph sensitivity. Keep simulator judgments separate from sampled HR@10.

References: [1] [Collaboration and Transition](https://arxiv.org/abs/2311.01056); [2] [Long/short-term modeling](https://journal.hep.com.cn/fcs/EN/10.1007/s11704-025-41181-y); [3] [eSASRec](https://arxiv.org/abs/2508.06450); [4] [SpotTarget](https://arxiv.org/abs/2306.00899); [5] [Dunning](https://aclanthology.org/J93-1003/).
