{
  "description": "Add an edge-reliability-weighted, bounded directed-transition residual to per-dataset SASRec. Aggregate training-derived directional evidence from distinct recent historical items, shrink edges using distinct-user support, and test the original requirement for two supporting sources as a separate ablation. Train with uniform multi-negative sampled softmax and compare against objective-only SASRec and the starting fixed lexical residual.",
  "motivation": "The user diagnosis identifies broad historical association and stale or isolated interactions as two concrete failures. Directional evidence, recency weighting, and independently replicated edge support address these failures as predictive heuristics. Multiple-source gating may further reduce isolated-source effects, but sparse coverage makes its benefit uncertain. Electronics has a negative lower-tail training margin and 16.04% negative-over-positive pairs, motivating an objective comparison without establishing the cause of validation errors. The supplied prior score of 0.5863 is contextual evidence, not a demonstrated result for this proposal. Compatibility, duplication, dimensions, quantity, and unmet needs remain largely unresolved because the available histories and text provide insufficient supervision for reliable labels.",
  "implementation_notes": "Protocol and scope: retain Movies_and_TV, Industrial_and_Scientific, Electronics, and CDs_and_Vinyl as separate models with unchanged routing and splits. Keep DATA_DIR = os.environ['GAGC_DATA_DIR'], preprocessing seed 0, training seed 42, and the effective SASREC_EPOCHS default 200. All artifacts remain beneath the trial directory. Use one GPU. Do not access test labels, extra datasets, pretrained weights, unavailable metadata, or simulator traits. Only permitted histories, timestamps, titles, and descriptions may be used. The generic metadata examples do not authorize ratings, reviews, categories, prices, or stores. No shell actions, training, or measurements are claimed in this report.\n\nTransition fitting: construct category-specific adjacent-transition counts C[a,b] exclusively from user_train histories in their existing chronological order. Count every adjacent nonpadding pair, including self-transitions; do not create edges across users. Let U[a,b] count distinct training users contributing that edge, counting each user once regardless of repeated occurrences. Let n[a] = sum_b C[a,b], d[b] = sum_a C[a,b], and T = sum_b d[b]. Over the N nonpadding items in the baseline training-visible catalog, define p0[b] = (d[b]+1)/(T+N). For tau=10, compute P(b|a) = (C[a,b]+tau*p0[b])/(n[a]+tau), L[a,b] = clip(log(P(b|a)/p0[b]), 0, 2), and E[a,b] = U[a,b]/(U[a,b]+2) * L[a,b]. Fix tau, clipping, and the distinct-user shrinkage constant before evaluation; do not tune them in the first family. For an unobserved edge, positive lift is zero, including a zero-outdegree source. Source reliability n[a]/(n[a]+tau) and edge reliability U[a,b]/(U[a,b]+2) address different uncertainties. Distinct users provide replication, not independent causal evidence. Save raw counts, user-support counts, priors, and resolved constants; retain raw edges even when their current positive lift is zero, because user-excluded diagnostics can change normalization.\n\nSparse implementation: never materialize an N-by-N matrix. Store raw edges in sorted CSR rows and optionally derive sorted int64 keys a*(N+1)+b for batched torch.searchsorted queries. Check insertion bounds before indexing, then verify exact key equality; absent keys return zero. Confirm the maximum key fits int64. CSR requires O(N+E_raw) storage, and sorted keys O(E_raw), excluding explicitly recorded per-user contributions. Chunk B-by-L-by-history-window-by-candidate queries to bound temporary memory. Full-catalog inference enumerates edges from at most 20 source rows, scatters evidence into candidate accumulators, and adds the residual to the existing collaborative score vector. Do not allocate all training-position-by-catalog scores. Measure sparse lookup and diagnostic cost within the search budget.\n\nPrefix construction: at each supervised position use only that position's observed prefix. Take its last 20 valid events, deduplicate by retaining each item's latest occurrence, and keep each retained event's original age, with age zero for the latest event. Define w[a] = exp(-age[a]/5)*n[a]/(n[a]+tau), D = sum_a w[a], z[b] = sum_a w[a]*E[a,b]/max(D,1e-8), c[b] = sum_a w[a]*1[E[a,b]>0]/max(D,1e-8), and k[b] = sum_a 1[E[a,b]>0]. Unsupported sources remain in D. The ungated residual is R0[b] = c[b]*tanh(z[b]); the gated residual is R2[b] = 1[k[b]>=2]*R0[b]. Confidence c remains outside the normalized evidence score. Its additional attenuation is intentional and must be assessed through actual residual magnitude. Empty histories, absent edges, and zero-weight histories return exactly zero; one-source histories return zero only in the gated condition. Repetitions of a single historical ID never satisfy the two-source gate. Item event age is not elapsed wall-clock time; timestamps are not otherwise used in this first experiment.\n\nScoring: s[b] = h dot item_emb[b] + alpha*R[b], where alpha = 2*sigmoid(a), initialized by a = log(0.05/0.95), giving alpha=0.1. Since R lies in [0