training, and scratch-trained text generalization remain future directions rather than an uncontrolled search grid. A transition-only scoring diagnostic can describe branch behavior, but it is not an equal-training-budget model comparison.\n\nBudget and scheduling: first subtract every earlier training, diagnostic, debugging, and failed-trial GPU-second from 43,200. If none has been consumed, reserve 7,200 GPU-seconds for diagnostics and parity checks and allocate at most 9,000 to each of the four main conditions, or 2,250 per category. Otherwise allocate at most one-sixth of the remaining budget to the diagnostic reserve and divide the remainder equally among conditions and categories. Existing charged work is not reset by this report. Run at most 200 epochs and respect the unchanged checkpoint schedule. Record actual exposure and GPU time; retain complete-epoch checkpoints and the baseline checkpoint rule without silently changing evaluation timing. If a cap prevents a valid four-category result, mark that condition incomplete and do not rank a partial macro score. This family compares budget-limited methods; unequal completed epochs remain a limitation. Do not compensate failed conditions with unreported extra tuning.\n\nSelection and uncertainty: use only the unchanged host protocol: 200 validation users/category, 99 unique negative candidates, evaluation seed 42, and equal-weight macro HR@10. Keep the incumbent on exact ties and preserve every trial result. Report all four category values, macro HR@10, exposure, and cost. One additional hit changes a category rate by 0.005 and the macro rate by 0.00125. If the host already exposes authorized per-user hit indicators, a paired category-stratified bootstrap can describe fixed-model uncertainty; otherwise state that such uncertainty is unavailable. Do not modify the evaluator, generate replacement validation candidates, claim seed robustness, or infer full-catalog quality from sampled HR@10. Multiple condition selection remains exploratory.\n\nTraining-target inclusion and shortcut diagnosis: full training-graph fitting is legitimate and differs from incorporating validation or test interactions, which is prohibited. However, a training target often contributes its own source-target edge, so both training margins and support coverage can be optimistic. On deterministic sampled training users, compare full-graph scores with scores from a graph excluding all contributions by that user. Subtract that user's adjacent counts from C and one from U for every edge the user contributed, then recompute affected source totals, destination totals, T, p0, smoothing, clipping, edge shrinkage, and source reliability. Keep catalog N fixed. Merely zeroing the current edge, retaining the original priors, or subtracting one occurrence is insufficient. Avoid pruning before this diagnostic; edges previously clipped to zero may become positive after normalization changes. Cache per-user sparse contributions and process users in bounded chunks. Use frozen trained weights and identical prefixes/candidates for paired comparisons. These ablations measure dependence on self-contributed graph structure and do not remove memorization in model weights or constitute validation leakage tests. Full cross-fitted retraining is stronger but outside the first family.\n\nFor user-excluded diagnostics, report paired changes in target score, positive-minus-negative margin, residual support, and rank within a fixed explicitly labeled diagnostic candidate set. Reuse supplied training-batch negatives where possible. If diagnostic ranks require extra candidates, generate unique training-only candidates with a separate analysis generator seeded 42, excluding prefix IDs and target, and document their count; never label the result as host validation HR@10. Separate raw-count buckets C=0, C=1, C=2–4, C>=5 and distinct-user buckets U=0, U=1, U=2–4, U>=5. Also report destination-frequency deciles, zero-support rate, one-source versus multiple-source coverage, and repeated-event prevalence. These expose rare-edge, popularity, and own-history shortcuts; they do not prove causal explanations. Validation support-bucket breakdowns are permissible only when authorized evaluator outputs already provide the required IDs and outcomes, without changing the evaluator.\n\nDiagnostic adapter: preserve DiagnosisProbe(view, device).run_full_diagnosis(train_loader), returning metrics and metric_definitions, and the loader contract (None, sequence, positive, negative), each tensor B x L. Keep view.model as the actual trained nn.Module and view.item_emb as its collaborative embedding. Have log2feats return a packed tensor containing collaborative features and the prefix context required by the selected scorer at each causal position. For C, pack the at-most-20 source IDs and original event ages; store IDs in a representation that round-trips exactly, such as float64 packing with explicit integer recovery and range checks, while casting h back to its model dtype for scoring. For B, pack the at-most-20 original event IDs and ages needed to reconstruct its fixed lexical support. Keep item embeddings and trainable states inside the actual module, and store graph/text artifacts as persisted nontrainable state loaded identically for training and inference. predict_score must handle position-aligned items and candidate lists using an explicit documented broadcasting contract, unpack context, and reproduce the actual fused score. Preserve user_train, meta_data, and review_data without inventing unavailable fields. Keep causal-prefix, finite-value, and training-margin probes; use fused scores. Add defined support coverage, residual magnitude, amplitude, and user-exclusion sensitivity metrics. Label embedding cosine alignment as descriptive, not proof of collapse. Return item-category breakdowns as unavailable with the stated reason.\n\nSimulator use: twenty real training users, five per category, receive full-catalog unseen-item recommendations under the native protocol. Exclude historical IDs. Assess broad association, stale or isolated evidence, duplication, unsupported use cases, and unassessable practical fit separately. Available titles and descriptions may support some distinctions, but do not manufacture compatibility, ownership, quantity requirements, satisfied needs, or latent traits. Simulator feedback remains diagnostic and cannot replace macro HR@10 selection.\n\nInference parity: update the predictor writer to emit the matching architecture and load category-specific weights, counts or derived sparse evidence, priors, vocabulary where applicable, configuration, and learned amplitude from artifacts beneath the trial directory. Preserve working/predict.py routing, truncation, padding, deduplication, event ages, and candidate order. Use the full permitted training graph for validation and final inference; never add held-out edges. Full-graph training and inference use the same graph, while user-excluded graphs are diagnostic ablations only. Verify generated-predictor score parity on deterministic training-visible fixtures for empty histories, repeated IDs, one source, two sources, absent edges, missing text, zero-outdegree items, searchsorted boundary keys, and mixed-category routing. Run probes on the actual trained architecture. No implementation, execution, successful parity check, or validation gain is claimed here.",
  "pseudocode": "CONFIG: categories=FOUR_CATEGORIES, preprocessing_seed=0, training_seed=42\nCONFIG: epochs=env_default_200, history_window=20, recency_scale=5\nCONFIG: tau=10, distinct_user_shrinkage=2, lift_clip=2, negatives=32\n\nfit_graph(training_histories, N):\n    C = counts_of_adjacent_pairs_within_each_training_user()\n    U = distinct_training_user_count_for_each_edge()\n    n = outgoing_counts(C)\n    d = destination_counts(C)\n    p0 = (d + 1) / (sum(d) + N)\n    for observed edge (a,b):\n        P = (C[a,b] + tau*p0[b]) / (n[a] + tau)\n        lift = clip(log(P / p0[b]), 0, 2)\n        E[a,b] = U[a,b] / (U[a,b] + 2) * lift\n    save_raw_counts_support_priors_and_sorted_sparse_edges()\n    return E, n\n\ntransition_residual(prefix, candidates, gate):\n    events = last_20_valid_events(prefix)\n    sources, ages = keep_latest_occurrence_per_ID_preserving_original_event_age(events)\n    w = exp(-ages/5) * n[sources]/(n[sources]+tau)\n    evidence = exact_sparse_lookup(E, sources, candidates, missing=0)\n    supported = evidence > 0\n    denominator = max(sum(w), 1e-8)\n    z = sum_sources(w * evidence) / denominator\n    confidence = sum_sources(w * supported) / denominator\n    R = confidence * tanh(z)\n    if gate:\n        R *= (sum_sources(supported) >= 2)\n    return R\n\nscore(prefix_features, candidates, condition):\n    h, context = unpack(prefix_features)\n    base = dot(h, item_emb(candidates))\n    if condition == A:\n        return base\n    if condition == B:\n        R = fixed_TFIDF_repeated_support_residual(context, candidates)\n    else:\n        R = transition_residual(context, candidates, gate=(condition == C2))\n    return base + 2*sigmoid(a)*R\n\nremaining = 43200 - all_previous_charged_GPU_seconds\nreserve = min(7200, remaining/6)\ncondition_cap = (remaining-reserve)/4\nfor condition in [A, B, C0, C2]:\n    for category in FOUR_CATEGORIES:\n        reset_training_seed(42)\n        initialize_same_backbone_and_dedicated_negative_generator(seed=42)\n        initialize_a(log(0.05/0.95)) if residual_enabled\n        for batch in unchanged_loader(category):\n            features = causal_log2feats_with_required_prefix_context(batch.sequence)\n            candidates, mask = positive_plus_32_unique_uniform_negatives(\n                exclude=padding_target_and_observed_prefix_only)\n            loss = eligible_position_mean(masked_CE(score(features,candidates), target=0))\n            optimize(loss)\n            preserve_checkpoint_schedule_and_respect_category_time_cap(condition_cap/4)\n        save_weights_config_sparse_artifacts_and_matching_predictor()\n        verify_generated_predictor_parity_on_training_visible_fixtures()\n        run_adapted_DiagnosisProbe_with_fused_scores()\n    if all_four_categories_have_valid_results:\n        record_unchanged_host_macro_validation_HR10()\n    else:\n        mark_condition_incomplete()\n\nfor sampled_training_user in deterministic_diagnostic_sample:\n    excluded_C = global_C - all_adjacent_counts_from_user\n    excluded_U = global_U - indicator_user_contributed_each_edge\n    excluded_graph = recompute_all_affected_normalization_and_evidence(excluded_C, excluded_U)\n    compare_frozen_model_scores_on_identical_prefixes_and_diagnostic_candidates(\n        full_graph, excluded_graph)\n    report_paired_margin_rank_support_changes_by_count_and_user_support_bucket()\n\nselect_complete_condition_by_pinned_macro_HR10_with_incumbent_tie_break()\n# Keep simulator judgments and training-graph diagnostics separate from validation metrics.\n# Held-out interactions never enter graph construction, exclusions, or calibration.",
