{
  "description": "Add a causal two-witness support residual to SASRec. Each historical anchor contributes only when two historical item identities, different from each other and the anchor, provide positionally separated lexical support. Confidence-weighted ID embeddings form a bounded residual retained during inference. Confidence denotes deterministic corroboration strength, not a calibrated probability. The residual represents history-level evidence; it does not enforce candidate-specific corroboration.",
  "motivation": "The supplied feedback identifies isolated interactions and broad associations as possible sources of irrelevant recommendations. Requiring distinct witnesses operationalizes recurring lexical support without inferring ownership, unmet needs, compatibility, ratings or simulator traits. Distinct IDs are not statistically independent evidence: variants, duplicate descriptions and boilerplate can satisfy the rule. HGN and HAM substantially precede historical pooling and reinforced interests, so the contribution is a precisely specified support rule and controlled evaluation. No validation improvement or causal interpretation of user preferences is established.",
  "implementation_notes": "Fit category-specific, frozen TF–IDF on titles and descriptions of unique training-history items only. Concatenate title, a newline and description; replace absent fields with empty strings. Use sklearn TfidfVectorizer with lowercase=True, analyzer='word', token_pattern=r'(?u)\\b\\w\\w+\\b', ngram_range=(1,2), min_df=2, max_df=1.0, max_features=20000, sublinear_tf=True, use_idf=True, smooth_idf=True, norm='l2' and stop_words=None. Process fitting documents in ascending item-ID order. Transform other publicly available item text using this frozen transform; no held-out interactions or labels enter fitting. Empty documents map to zero; an empty fitted vocabulary disables the branch for that category. Serialize configuration, library version, vocabulary, IDF and item mapping beneath the trial directory. Keep the transformed item matrix sparse and avoid a dense catalog-by-catalog similarity matrix.\n\nFor each retained causal prefix ending at t, anchor j has confidence c[j,t] equal to the maximum of min(sim(j,k),sim(j,l)) across valid witnesses k<l<=t. Require three different item IDs for j,k,l and l-k>=3 in retained-event positions. All anchors must satisfy j<=t. Witnesses may precede or follow the anchor within the observed prefix; they need only be separated from each other. This precise rule does not require separation from the anchor. Spacing three and recency timescale twenty are fixed hypotheses, not established behavioral timescales. Nonnegative cosine and zero-vector handling make missing evidence contribute zero. Recompute support after truncation; no departing event may remain a witness. Use the baseline's retained events, padding and learned-position indexing. Positional ages and witness spacing use ranks among retained valid events, not timestamps or padding indices.\n\nCompute all prefix confidences exactly in quadratic work after obtaining the sequence similarity matrix. For each anchor row a[q]=sim(j,q), scan right witness l in chronological order. Before querying l, insert k=l-3 when valid and its item ID differs from the anchor. Maintain the largest per-label maxima for two distinct item IDs, with their witness indices; update an existing retained label by its maximum, or compare a newly encountered label with the two retained entries. Two entries suffice because each query excludes only the right witness's label, with the anchor label already filtered. Query the largest retained maximum whose label differs from item[l], skip l when it equals the anchor ID, and update running_best with min(a[l], eligible_max). Save running_best as c[j,l] only when anchor j exists in that prefix. Missing eligible witnesses give confidence zero because similarities are nonnegative. Resolve equal maxima by ascending item ID and then earliest witness position. This is a direct algebraic consequence of max_k min(a[k],a[l])=min(max_k a[k],a[l]); the supplied streaming-extrema reference is background, not evidence for this exact labeled algorithm. Blocking cubic pair enumeration only reduces memory and is unnecessary here.\n\nEach anchor scan is O(L), giving O(L²) scan work per sequence. The similarity matrix and stored confidence matrix require O(L²) memory; sparse cosine computation has additional feature-dependent cost. A naive Python loop over anchors, prefixes and batch elements can still be slow. Use batched tensor scans over the prefix axis, or precompute exact deterministic confidences for an explicitly fixed finite set of training windows. Do not assume GPU efficiency from asymptotic complexity. Profile the implementation and count profiling, diagnostics and failures against the shared budget. Compare the optimized scan with brute-force pair enumeration on small synthetic sequences before training. Do not silently introduce top-neighbor truncation or compressed text projections, which change the algorithm.\n\nSet w[j,t]=c[j,t]*exp(-(t-j)/20), using retained-event ranks for t-j. Use the raw trainable item embedding table, without input dropout or the SASRec input sqrt(D) multiplier, for every pooling arm. Define p[t]=sum_j(w[j,t]*embedding[j])/(1+sum_j(w[j,t])). Define RMS(v)=sqrt(mean(v²)+1e-8), r[t]=p[t]*min(1,detach(RMS(base[t]))/RMS(p[t])). Return base[t]+tanh(alpha)*r[t], with one category-specific scalar alpha initialized to zero and padding outputs masked. Weak evidence is never scaled upward. Gradients flow through pooled embeddings and their norm, while the cap's base-feature norm is detached. The scalar gate can become negative; report its learned sign rather than interpreting it automatically as positive interest reinforcement. At initialization the branch gives baseline-equivalent features, its gate can receive gradients, and shared embeddings continue receiving baseline gradients. Preserve the baseline scoring convention identically during training and export.\n\nPreserve DATA_DIR=os.environ['GAGC_DATA_DIR'], preprocessing seed 0, training seed 42, SASREC_EPOCHS environment default 200, original splits, four dataset routes and single-GPU execution. Keep baseline loss, optimizer and other settings for the first comparison. Do not assume a new sampler from the diagnostic loader's supplied negative items. Before executing trials, record the exact baseline settings, starting-residual configuration, checkpoint cadence, checkpoint selection rule, arm order and GPU-second allocations in a trial manifest. Those baseline details and measured runtimes are not supplied here, so a fully executable training reproduction cannot honestly be inferred from this report alone. Count every attempted configuration, failure, diagnostic and evaluation against 43,200 GPU-seconds. Do not reduce training allowance for controls after observing their scores.\n\nRetain unchanged SASRec, the starting residual and the revised residual as the primary comparison. Use a matched recency-only residual with c=1 and otherwise identical denominator, cap, gate, embedding convention and training allowance to test whether corroboration contributes beyond pooling. Use a whole-text reassignment control, seeded at zero, within each dataset and joint strata defined by training-frequency decile, whitespace-token-count decile and metadata availability. Compute decile assignments using training-history items with stable item-ID tie breaking; leave singleton strata unchanged and report the fraction actually reassigned. Refit the same TF–IDF specification on the reassigned training documents for that arm. Nontraining items retain their public text and use the resulting frozen transform. This control is approximate because matching bins are coarse and singleton strata cannot be shuffled. Word-order shuffling is insufficient: it preserves unigram counts, although it can change the specified bigrams. Do not claim that all controls fit the budget before runtime accounting; identify incomplete comparisons explicitly.\n\nTreat title-only, description-only, boilerplate-masked text, uniform pooling, most-recent-item similarity, HAM-style synergy, one-versus-two witnesses, relaxed identity constraints and duplicate handling as optional separately budgeted follow-ups. Define any duplicate detector, boilerplate-removal rule, additional hyperparameter and matching tolerance before using it. The Amazon documentation motivates auditing variants but does not establish their presence in these splits. Report descriptive training-text duplicate rates first; avoid inferring semantic independence from distinct IDs. Matched unrelated-user histories and position/order perturbations are auxiliary dependence probes, not substitutes for untouched benchmark histories or evidence of user causality. Text reassignment, history order and popularity controls answer different questions.\n\nUse only the pinned host macro validation HR@10 for selection: 200 validation users per category, 99 unique negative candidates and evaluation seed 42. Report each category and the unweighted macro mean as sampled-candidate outcomes. If the host exposes paired per-user hits, use 10,000 seed-42 bootstrap resamples independently within categories to estimate uncertainty in paired macro differences; retain checkpoint and candidate alignment. Otherwise omit intervals. These intervals do not cover training-seed variability or adaptive checkpoint/trial selection. Prespecify history-length and target training-frequency strata for descriptive reporting without subgroup tuning. Auxiliary full-catalog scores and twenty-user simulator judgments do not replace this objective. Do not access hidden test files, download external weights or acquire additional datasets.\n\nUpdate the predictor writer to serialize and execute the same architecture, text mapping, masks, raw pooling embeddings, positional convention and retained-prefix computation. In evaluation mode, verify training-side and exported predictor score parity with identical checkpoints and candidates, using atol=1e-5 and rtol=1e-4 for float32 calculations. Run checks for all four routes, repeated IDs, missing text, no eligible witnesses, ties, padding, one-event prefixes, truncated windows and all-padding inputs. Verify that removing events after a boundary leaves earlier features unchanged and that disabling the branch reproduces baseline features. Final scoring must use the baseline's final valid-position convention.\n\nPreserve DiagnosisProbe(view, device).run_full_diagnosis(train_loader), returning metrics and metric_definitions, with view.model pointing to the actual trained nn.Module. view.item_emb, log2feats(sequence) and predict_score(features, items) must expose the trained architecture; user_train, meta_data and review_data retain compatibility while unavailable fields remain unavailable. The loader yields (None, sequence, positive, negative), each tensor B x L. Restore model modes and Python, NumPy, Torch and initialized CUDA RNG states after probes. Keep alignment and training-margin metrics descriptive rather than treating them as validated quality measures. Twenty real training users, five per category, receive full-catalog unseen-item recommendations for descriptive inspection only. Missing item categories, ratings, reviews, prices, stores, ownership and simulator traits must never be inferred or fabricated; compatibility claims require explicit title/description evidence and otherwise remain unknown.",
  "pseudocode": "Prespecify baseline configuration, arms, controls, checkpoint rule and accounted GPU budget\nFor each category:\n    fit frozen TFIDF on unique training-history items in stable item-ID order\n    serialize transform, sparse item-text matrix, item mapping and configuration\n    initialize unchanged SASRec and scalar alpha = 0\n    for sequence, positive, negative in baseline training batches:\n        base = SASRec.log2feats(sequence)\n        gather retained valid events using baseline truncation and padding\n        S = nonnegative_cosine(frozen_text(events)); zero missing-text similarities\n        for each anchor j:\n            top_two = empty distinct-label prefix maxima\n            best = 0\n            for right witness l in chronological event order:\n                k = l - 3\n                if k exists and item[k] != item[j]:\n                    insert/update (item[k], S[j,k], k) in top_two\n                if item[l] != item[j]:\n                    v = largest retained maximum with label != item[l]\n                    if v exists: best = max(best, min(v, S[j,l]))\n                confidence[j,l] = best if j <= l else 0\n        for each valid prefix ending at t:\n            w[j] = confidence[j,t] * exp(-(t-j)/20), for j <= t\n            pooled = sum(w[j] * raw_item_embedding(item[j])) / (1 + sum(w))\n            rms_pool = sqrt(mean(pooled**2) + 1e-8)\n            rms_base = detach(sqrt(mean(base[t]**2) + 1e-8))\n            cap = min(1, rms_base / rms_pool)\n            features[t] = base[t] + tanh(alpha) * cap * pooled\n        mask padding features\n        optimize unchanged baseline objective with features\n    export identical architecture and feature computation through predictor writer\nVerify exact scan against brute-force pairs and verify exported-score parity\nRun prespecified comparisons and controls only within accounted shared budget\nSelect solely using pinned host macro validation HR@10\nReport measured per-category