2026-10-23 08:39:55,025 self_evolverec INFO   Report 1: At 20% progress, prioritize a small, controlled extension: **anchor repeated lexical support to recent activity**.

Starting-point insights:
- Distinct-item agreement prevents repetitions of one item from activating support. It still allows an older coherent cluster to influence recommendations.
- Applying confidence after pool normalization preserves evidence strength. Normalization alone can amplify weak agreement into a unit-length preference vector.
- Electronics has 16.04% negative-over-positive training pairs and a negative tenth-percentile margin. This motivates objective controls, but does not establish the cause of validation errors.

Related-work insights:
- [ICSRec](https://doi.org/10.1145/3616855.3635773) motivates separating intentions within histories. Agreement between subsequences remains a proxy for intent.
- [Hierarchical long/short-term modeling](https://journal.hep.com.cn/fcs/EN/10.1007/s11704-025-41181-y) matches historical preferences against recent behavior. This suggests testing recent anchoring before introducing learned routing.
- [gSASRec](https://www.ijcai.org/proceedings/2024/0939.pdf) and [sampled-softmax analysis](https://wujcan.github.io/papers/tois-ssm.pdf) motivate objective comparisons. Neither establishes gains under this evaluator.

These insights organize into three directions: evidence specificity, temporal selection, and candidate discrimination. The framework below separates their roles:

| Method | Evidence | Temporal selection | Objective |
|---|---|---|---|
| SASRec | Learned item interactions | Causal attention | Baseline BCE |
| Starting residual | Repeated lexical agreement | Recency decay | Multi-negative CE |
| Proposed residual | Repeated agreement linked to recent items | Recent anchors plus decay | Same CE |

Three candidate ideas follow. Scores are originality/potential/user alignment/model alignment/difficulty; higher difficulty means harder implementation.

| Idea | Scores | Positive assessment | Negative assessment |
|---|---|---|---|
| Recent-anchored lexical support | 4/7/8/6/4 | Directly targets broad association and stale interests; preserves an inspectable branch | Incremental; lexical overlap can miss useful complements and attenuation may erase signal |
| Generalized-BCE control | 2/7/3/8/2 | Cheap, established response to sampled-loss weaknesses | Does not explicitly resolve two user-facing failures, so cannot be selected alone |
| Learned recent/history routing | 4/7/8/6/7 | Can express multiple persistent preferences and recent changes | Adds parameters and ambiguity before simple controls establish value |

**Selected algorithm.** Keep fixed training-only TF–IDF vectors. For each causal prefix, use the last twenty events and the last three events as recent anchors. Historical evidence receives weight only through lexical agreement with distinct historical items and distinct recent anchors. Multiply the normalized pooled score by its unnormalized support confidence.

This explicitly targets broad matches lacking repeated specific evidence and older or isolated interactions carrying forward. Shared multi-negative training addresses the observed weak pair separation as a hypothesis; it cannot guarantee improvement. Compatibility, duplication, and unmet needs remain unresolved without reliable labels.

Compare objective-only SASRec, the starting residual, and anchored support under equal remaining-budget caps. Select complete four-category runs solely by pinned macro validation HR@10. Inspect fused training margins, support activation, residual scale, and actual rank changes. Prior supplied scores are contextual observations, not evidence that this proposal improves performance.
2026-10-23 08:39:55,025 self_evolverec INFO   Report 2: At 20% progress, prioritize a controlled test of **recent-anchored lexical support**, retaining simpler pooling and objective-only alternatives as eligible winners.

Starting-point insights:
- Distinct-ID exclusion prevents self-matches, but maximum similarity measures strongest resemblance rather than recurrence frequency. Different IDs can also represent near-duplicate editions.
- Confidence outside normalization preserves evidence strength. However, multiplicative agreement can suppress useful signals through concentration alone.
- Electronics has 16.04% negative-over-positive training pairs and a negative tenth-percentile margin. This motivates objective controls without explaining validation errors.

Related-work insights:
- [ICSRec](https://doi.org/10.1145/3616855.3635773) motivates separating intentions across subsequences. Shared-target alignment remains an intent proxy.
- [Hierarchical long/short-term modeling](https://journal.hep.com.cn/fcs/EN/10.1007/s11704-025-41181-y) and [PAN](https://arxiv.org/abs/2006.15346) motivate matching historical preferences to multiple recent events. Neither validates lexical gates.
- [SIM](https://arxiv.org/abs/2006.05639) selects candidate-relevant history. Its candidate-conditioned query differs from the proposed recent-history query.
- [gSASRec](https://arxiv.org/abs/2308.07192) motivates calibrated BCE with more negatives. Loss and negative-count effects require separate controls.

These insights organize into evidence specificity, temporal selection, and candidate discrimination:

| Method | Evidence | Temporal selection | Objective |
|---|---|---|---|
| SASRec | Learned interactions | Causal attention | Baseline BCE |
| Starting residual | Distinct-item lexical resemblance | Recency decay | Multi-negative CE |
| Proposed residual | Resemblance linked to recent distinct IDs | Recent anchors plus decay | Same CE |

Three candidate ideas follow. Scores are originality/potential/user alignment/model alignment/difficulty; higher difficulty means harder implementation. Detailed positive and negative reasons appear in implementation notes.

| Idea | Scores | Positive assessment | Negative assessment |
|---|---|---|---|
| Recent-anchored lexical support | 3/6/7/5/5 | Targets broad association and stale evidence through an inspectable comparison | Incremental; extra attenuation may explain any apparent benefit |
| Generalized-BCE control | 2/7/3/8/3 | Established, inexpensive discrimination alternative | Does not explicitly address two user failures; ineligible as the selected research idea alone |
| Learned recent/history routing | 4/7/8/6/7 | Expresses multiple persistent preferences and recent changes | More parameters and ambiguity before simpler controls establish value |

**Selected algorithm.** Preserve fixed training-only TF–IDF. Within each causal twenty-event prefix, compute distinct-ID whole-window agreement u and agreement a with the last three events. Pool with weights r·u·a and apply support confidence after normalization. Because anchors are a subset of the window, a≤u: compare against r·u² before attributing gains to recent relevance.

The proposal explicitly targets broad associations lacking specific evidence and older or isolated interactions carrying forward. Shared multi-negative training targets weak sampled pair separation; neither mechanism establishes practical compatibility or unmet needs.

Compare objective-only SASRec, starting support, anchored support, and squared-support control under equal remaining-budget caps. Include recent-only and random/old-anchor frozen-model diagnostics. Select complete four-category runs solely by pinned macro validation HR@10. Inspect support, effective pooling size, duplicate concentration, fused margins, and rank changes. Historical scores and literature results establish no improvement for this proposal.
