  "motivation": "The selected idea explicitly addresses two diagnosed user failures: broad historical associations outranking more specific matches, and older or isolated interactions carrying forward despite recent activity. Distinct-ID lexical resemblance supplies a limited specificity proxy, while recent-anchor agreement suppresses disconnected historical clusters. Uniform multi-negative training targets the model-facing weak sampled training-pair separation, especially Electronics, as a hypothesis. Maximum resemblance does not measure recurrence frequency, and distinct IDs do not guarantee distinct product families. Neither histories nor lexical agreement establish compatibility, ownership, quantity requirements, satisfied needs, complements, or simulator traits.",
  "implementation_notes": "Protocol: retain Movies_and_TV, Industrial_and_Scientific, Electronics, and CDs_and_Vinyl as separate category models with unchanged baseline routing and splits. Preserve preprocessing seed 0, training seed 42, DATA_DIR = os.environ['GAGC_DATA_DIR'], single-GPU execution, and effective SASREC_EPOCHS default 200. Save checkpoints, text artifacts, configuration, diagnostics, and generated working/predict.py beneath the trial directory. Use only permitted histories, titles, descriptions, and timestamps. Generic examples do not authorize review ratings/text or item ratings/categories/prices/stores. Do not access hidden test files, fetch datasets or pretrained weights, or modify the host evaluator. No implementation, execution, successful parity check, or validation improvement is claimed.\n\nText features: preserve the starting implementation's deterministic training-visible TF–IDF preprocessing for attribution. If implementing anew, use lowercase Unicode alphanumeric tokens; compute document frequency over complete permitted title-plus-description documents from items occurring in training histories. Define N as that training-item count. Build at most 30,000 vocabulary entries, ordered by descending document frequency with lexical tie-breaking, excluding tokens with df/N > 0.20. Retain the first 32 surviving tokens from title followed by description, collapse repeated tokens to binary term frequency, and apply IDF = log((1+N)/(1+df))+1. L2-normalize rows with epsilon 1e-8; missing or filtered text yields exactly zero. Map items outside the fitted training-item scope to zero text rows rather than using held-out interactions to extend the vocabulary. Persist vocabulary, document frequencies, IDF, filtering, row mappings, catalog scope, tokenizer rules, and truncation order. Record any difference from an existing incumbent; a changed preprocessing pipeline requires a newly matched starting comparator.\n\nCausal features: at each supervised position use the sequence through that position, excluding its shifted next-item target and future events. Take its last twenty nonpadding events, retaining event order and repeated IDs. Define anchors as its last three events, without extending backward when IDs repeat. For event j, r_j=exp(-age_j/5), where age zero denotes the latest event. Let u_j be maximum nonnegative TF–IDF cosine to any window event with a different item ID, and a_j maximum cosine to any anchor with a different item ID; empty eligible sets return zero. Mask all equal-ID matches, not merely diagonal positions. Missing-text events supply zero agreement but remain in the confidence denominator. Define w_j=r_j*u_j*a_j, q=normalize_or_zero(sum_j w_j*v_j), and c=sum_j w_j/max(sum_j r_j,1e-8). Repeated occurrences of one ID cannot activate support alone, but can amplify support supplied by another ID. Explicitly measure that limitation.\n\nDependence and controls: anchors are a subset of the window, so 0 <= a_j <= u_j <= 1. Thus u_j*a_j <= u_j²; the two agreement factors are dependent, and their product is not evidence from two independent sources. Primary conditions are A: objective-only SASRec; B: starting support w=r*u; C: anchored support w=r*u*a; D: squared support w=r*u². For B, C, and D use the same q normalization and c=sum(w)/sum(r). C versus B combines temporal selection with attenuation; C versus D is a necessary stronger concentration control, although it still does not match confidence or effective pooling size exactly. Do not tune an exponent to match validation outcomes after inspecting them. Record normalized weights p_j=w_j/sum(w), effective event pool size 1/sum(p_j²), and effective distinct-ID pool size after aggregating p by ID; report unavailable when support mass is zero. Maxima represent strongest resemblance, not a count of independent recurring interests.\n\nScoring: s_i=h dot e_i + alpha*c*(q dot v_i), alpha=2*A*sigmoid(b), initialized with sigmoid(b)=0.05. Calibrate category-specific A=max(1,median absolute collaborative positive-minus-uniform-negative margin) using the first 10,000 eligible training positions, or all available if fewer, from a compatible frozen baseline checkpoint in deterministic order. Record the checkpoint, ordered positions, exclusions, sampled IDs, and separate calibration generator seed 42; charge all work to the budget. Share A across residual conditions within category. If preserving a different incumbent calibration, use it for all residual conditions and document it rather than changing calibration and anchoring simultaneously. If no compatible baseline checkpoint exists, predeclare A=1 for every residual condition and label this a fixed-scale experiment, avoiding an undeclared baseline training expense. Since c is in [0,1] and cosine is in [-1,1], pairwise residual changes are bounded by 2*alpha*c for a fixed prefix. These bounds do not guarantee useful scale; report alpha, c, residual variance, residual-to-collaborative-margin scale, and actual rank changes.\n\nObjective and sampling: use 32 distinct uniform negatives per valid target in all four primary conditions. Exclude padding, target, and IDs observed in that causal prefix only; never consult future interactions or held-out labels. Draw by uniform rejection sampling without replacement using a dedicated generator seeded 42. Use every eligible item if fewer than 32 exist, mask unused slots, and omit positions with no eligible negative. Save or deterministically regenerate candidate streams shared across conditions. Optimize cross-entropy with positive index zero and temperature 1, averaging over eligible positions. No LogQ correction is used: this is a sampled ranking surrogate, not an unbiased full-softmax estimator. Observed-positive filtering does not eliminate latent false negatives. Keep backbone, optimizer, learning rate, regularization, batch size, sequence length, loader ordering, validation schedule, and checkpoint rule unchanged; record resolved settings from the host template. Separate model and sampling RNG streams; initialize shared backbone weights identically. Do not add semi-hard mining, learned text encoders, auxiliary losses, or architecture changes in this experiment.\n\nObjective alternatives: BCE, generalized BCE, and sampled softmax remain plausible follow-up winners; no cited paper establishes a universal choice here. The primary family holds sampled CE and negative count fixed to isolate residual variants. It cannot establish that sampled CE caused improvement over the historical BCE baseline. Any later objective comparison must use identical architectures, uniform candidate pools, negative counts, batching, and exposure, and implement the cited gSASRec calibration equation with its exact resolved parameters recorded. Vary negative count separately. A full architecture-by-objective factorial is deferred because it would dilute the present budget; do not claim its interactions have been measured. Loss-only alternatives remain eligible deployment choices even though they do not independently meet the selected research idea's two-user-failure requirement.\n\nSparse implementation: store normalized item rows in CSR and avoid any item-by-item matrix. For each prefix, obtain only its at-most-20-row Gram matrix and recent-anchor columns, mask equal IDs, and construct the weighted sparse sum. Cache fixed context features per training-visible sequence position when storage and sequence construction permit; otherwise process bounded batches and cache repeated windows. Candidate scoring gathers candidate text rows and computes sparse dot products; detach only fixed text state, preserving gradient to alpha. A simple rolling sum is insufficient because arrival or departure of an event can change maximum agreement and therefore the weights of all retained events. Recompute these weights or maintain a correctly tested dynamic maximum structure. Profile preprocessing, sparse operations, transfers, and chunk