d competitors; they can diagnose sensitivity but cannot establish which pooling rule would win after training. Training such alternatives requires a separately declared, charged follow-up family. A timestamp-value shuffle cannot affect this first algorithm because it uses event age rather than elapsed time; do not present that null intervention as evidence of temporal robustness. Shuffling event order changes sequential inputs and has different confounds, so it is excluded from the initial checks.\n\nDuplicates and lexical shortcuts: measure repeated-ID frequency and training-only normalized-title duplicates. Normalize titles using the same lowercase tokenization and compare complete normalized titles, excluding empty strings; these are exact lexical groups, not verified product families. Aggregate support mass by event, distinct ID, and title group, and report concentration alongside residual magnitude. On the same frozen checkpoint, suppress equal-title cross-item matches and recompute the entire context, then compare support, scores, margins, and rank changes on identical diagnostic fixtures. Also inspect title-only, description-only, and removal of the most frequent retained tokens as predeclared frozen-feature perturbations where reserve permits; rebuild normalization consistently and report all outcomes. These interventions alter feature quality and scale and are not retraining controls. Approximate description clustering and one-vote-per-cluster training remain future experiments requiring a specified clustering algorithm and threshold. Amazon's older dataset documentation establishes a plausible near-duplicate mechanism, not its prevalence in this pinned benchmark. Do not modify input splits or introduce an internal chronological holdout; use training-only diagnostic fixtures and the existing authorized validation protocol.\n\nSelection and uncertainty: use only pinned validation with 200 users/category, 99 unique negative candidates, evaluation seed 42, and