omparator with an unsuitable amplitude can exaggerate masking gains. Small validation improvements do not justify claiming a specificity mechanism.\n\nLow-cost diagnostic framework: on the same frozen compatible checkpoint, compare last-item cosine, normalized history centroid, exponential-recency centroid, recurrence-only centroid, and the selected profile. Separately remove the corpus-frequency filter and the recurrence filter. These are inference interventions, not equally trained competitors. For scale analysis, use deterministic training-visible fixtures and a common candidate list per prefix to center each residual over candidates and calculate its RMS. Where both RMS values exceed 1e-8, rescale masked residuals to the unmasked RMS; report zero-RMS cases separately without division or arbitrary replacement. This fixture-only intervention isolates some magnitude effects but changes the deployed rule and does not establish retrained validation superiority.\n\nRandom-mask control: for each prefix, partition nonzero profile dimensions by floor(log2(max(df,1))). In each bin, randomly retain the same number of dimensions retained by the proposed gate, using a separate analysis generator seeded 42 and a fixed fixture order. This matches retained feature count and coarse document-frequency distribution, not exact removed mass or semantics. Use ten predeclared random realizations if the diagnostic reserve permits; otherwise declare the smaller count before execution. Compare removed norm, centered candidate residual RMS, support activation, and rank changes. Do not select a favorable realization. These controls and RMS matching are diagnostic unless separately trained and charged.\n\nShortcut and duplication audits: compute exact groups from complete lowercase whitespace-normalized titles and descriptions, excluding empty strings. For a predeclared near-duplicate diagnostic, use training-fitted character 3-5-gram TF–IDF and cosine >=0.95, comparing only the bounded his