ration. Preserve chronological occurrences; repeated IDs cannot corroborate one another, but multiple occurrences can still increase aggregate pooling mass.\n\nFor j<=i, the combined decay equals exp(-(t-i)/rho_eff), where 1/rho_eff=1/rho_anchor+1/rho_witness; the proposed defaults give rho_eff=5. Only later witnesses distinguish the operator from this reduction. A recent anchor with an ancient witness has pair freshness one, although the spacing constraint still applies. Report these behaviors explicitly. If actual witness age is desired instead, exp(-(t-j)/rho_witness) defines a different experiment and must not be silently substituted.\n\nCompute p=sum_i(weight_i*embedding_i)/(1+sum_i(weight_i)), then multiply p by min(1,stop_gradient(norm(h))/(norm(p)+epsilon)). Never normalize weak support upward. Return h+0.25*tanh(g)*p with one scalar g=0 per category. Empty support returns exactly zero. The cap bounds residual norm relative to the current backbone feature but does not calibrate relevance or confidence. Negative learned gates are permitted and must be reported. Preserve the baseline objective, optimizer, batching, sampler, initialization, and checkpoint protocol for the first comparisons.\n\nUse sequence-local sparse text products, avoiding catalog-by-catalog similarities. The pseudocode is a correctness oracle. For a fixed retained window, an exact vectorized implementation can use transformed keys S(i,j)*exp(max(i,j)/rho_witness) with a cumulative maximum over j, followed by exp(-t/rho_witness), causal masks, and anchor decay. Rebase indices to the local window and use float64 intermediate keys or log-domain comparisons to avoid overflow; zero scores have log key negative infinity. Verify final float32 outputs against the oracle. The support and embedding aggregation cost O(BL²) and O(BL²d), respectively, excluding sparse similarity construction. Never build a B x L x L x L tensor.\n\nIf inference instead maintains sliding state, a single maximum per anchor